S={
"Hammurabi":"Sixth king of the First Dynasty of Babylon (r. c. 1792–1750 BCE), he turned a small city-state into an empire covering most of Mesopotamia through diplomacy and war against Larsa, Eshnunna and Mari. Late in his reign he set up stone stelae carrying 282 laws, framed as given by the sun god Shamash, so that 'the strong may not oppress the weak'. His letters show a king closely involved in canals, taxes and court cases.",
"Ramesses II":"The 'Great Ancestor' of later pharaohs, Ramesses II reigned for 66 years (c. 1279–1213 BCE). He fought the Hittites at Kadesh (c. 1274 BCE), proclaimed it a victory, and later signed the world's oldest surviving peace treaty with them. He built Abu Simbel, the Ramesseum and a new capital, Pi-Ramesses, and fathered over a hundred children.",
"Hatshepsut":"Daughter of Thutmose I, Hatshepsut ruled first as regent for her stepson Thutmose III and then as pharaoh (c. 1479–1458 BCE), depicted with the royal false beard. Her reign was peaceful and prosperous: she sent an expedition to the land of Punt for incense and built the terraced temple at Deir el-Bahari. After her death her images were chiselled away, perhaps to secure Thutmose III's line.",
"Cyrus the Great":"Founder of the Achaemenid Persian Empire (r. c. 559–530 BCE), Cyrus overthrew the Medes, conquered Lydia and took Babylon in 539 BCE almost without a fight. The Cyrus Cylinder records that he returned deported peoples and restored their temples; the Bible praises him for letting the Jews return to Jerusalem. He died campaigning in Central Asia and was buried in a simple tomb at Pasargadae.",
"Alexander the Great":"King of Macedon at 20, Alexander (356–323 BCE) was taught by Aristotle and in 11 years conquered the Persian Empire, Egypt and lands to the Indus, never losing a major battle. At the Hydaspes (326 BCE) he defeated Porus, but his exhausted army refused to go beyond the Beas. He founded cities, mixed Greek and Persian elites and spread Greek culture, but died in Babylon at 32, and his empire split among his generals.",
"Chandragupta Maurya":"With the guidance of his mentor Chanakya, Chandragupta (r. c. 322–297 BCE) overthrew the Nanda dynasty and founded the Maurya Empire, the first to unite most of the Indian subcontinent. He defeated Seleucus I, who ceded the northwest in exchange for 500 elephants. Jain tradition says he abdicated, became a monk under Bhadrabahu and fasted to death at Shravanabelagola.",
"Ashoka":"Grandson of Chandragupta, Ashoka (r. c. 268–232 BCE) conquered Kalinga in a war that his own edict says killed 100,000 and deported 150,000. Filled with remorse, he embraced Buddhism and dhamma — non-violence, tolerance and welfare — building hospitals, wells and rest houses and sending missions abroad. His lion capital at Sarnath is India's national emblem and his wheel is on its flag.",
"Qin Shi Huang":"King Ying Zheng of Qin conquered the six rival Warring States and in 221 BCE proclaimed himself First Emperor. He standardised script, coins, weights and roads, joined border walls into an early Great Wall, and ordered the burning of books and the persecution of scholars. Obsessed with immortality, he died in 210 BCE and was buried with the Terracotta Army.",
"Julius Caesar":"A Roman general and politician (100–44 BCE), Caesar conquered Gaul, crossed the Rubicon in 49 BCE to start a civil war, and became dictator. He reformed the calendar (the Julian calendar) and expanded the Senate. Senators who feared he would become king stabbed him on the Ides of March, 44 BCE; his heir Octavian became Rome's first emperor.",
"Augustus":"Born Octavian, Caesar's adopted heir defeated Antony and Cleopatra at Actium (31 BCE) and in 27 BCE became Augustus, the first Roman emperor. He kept republican forms while holding real power, began 200 years of the Pax Romana, built roads and temples, and said he found Rome brick and left it marble. The month of August is named after him.",
"Cleopatra":"Cleopatra VII (69–30 BCE), last of the Ptolemies and the only one to learn Egyptian, allied with Julius Caesar and then Mark Antony to preserve Egypt's independence. After their defeat at Actium, she took her own life rather than be paraded in Rome. Egypt became a Roman province.",
"Kanishka":"The greatest Kushan emperor (r. c. 127–150 CE), Kanishka ruled from Bactria to the Ganga with capitals at Purushapura (Peshawar) and Mathura. A patron of Buddhism, he convened the Fourth Buddhist Council in Kashmir and supported Gandhara art. His coins show Greek, Persian, Indian and Buddhist gods.",
"Samudragupta":"The Gupta emperor (r. c. 335–375 CE) described in the Allahabad pillar inscription as having defeated kings across north India and made southern rulers tributaries. He also performed the horse sacrifice and was depicted on coins playing the veena, showing his love of music and poetry.",
"Justinian I":"Byzantine emperor (r. 527–565) who tried to restore the Roman Empire, reconquering North Africa and Italy. He codified Roman law in the Corpus Juris Civilis, rebuilt Hagia Sophia, and survived the Nika riots thanks to his wife Theodora. His reign was also struck by the Plague of Justinian.",
"Harsha":"Harshavardhana (r. 606–647) unified north India from Kannauj, wrote Sanskrit plays (Ratnavali, Nagananda), and hosted the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang. Pulakeshin II stopped his southern expansion at the Narmada.",
"Charlemagne":"King of the Franks (r. 768–814), crowned emperor by Pope Leo III in 800, he united much of western Europe, conquered the Saxons and Lombards, and sponsored the Carolingian Renaissance of learning and script.",
"Rajaraja Chola I":"Chola emperor (r. 985–1014) who conquered the Cheras, Pandyas and northern Sri Lanka, built a navy, carried out a land survey, and built the Brihadeeswarar temple at Thanjavur (1010).",
"Genghis Khan":"Born Temüjin (c. 1162–1227), he united the Mongol tribes in 1206 and conquered from China to Persia, creating the largest land empire. He used merit-based promotion, a postal system and religious tolerance, but his conquests killed millions.",
"Razia Sultan":"The only woman to rule the Delhi Sultanate (1236–1240), she wore a man's clothes, led armies and appointed non-Turkic officials, angering nobles who overthrew and killed her.",
"Mansa Musa":"Emperor of Mali (r. c. 1312–1337), perhaps the richest person in history. His hajj in 1324 made Mali famous, and he built mosques and libraries in Timbuktu.",
"Krishnadevaraya":"Vijayanagara emperor (r. 1509–1529) who defeated the Deccan sultans and Gajapatis, patronised Telugu and Kannada poets (the Ashtadiggajas) and wrote Amuktamalyada.",
"Babur":"A descendant of Timur and Genghis Khan, Babur lost his homeland Fergana, took Kabul, and defeated Ibrahim Lodi at Panipat (1526) with field guns, founding the Mughal Empire. His memoir, the Baburnama, is frank and vivid.",
"Akbar":"The greatest Mughal emperor (r. 1556–1605), Akbar expanded the empire across north India, abolished the jizya tax on non-Muslims, married Rajput princesses, held interfaith debates in the Ibadat Khana and created a revenue system under Todar Mal.",
"Shah Jahan":"Mughal emperor (r. 1628–1658) who built the Taj Mahal for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, the Red Fort and Jama Masjid in Delhi. His son Aurangzeb imprisoned him in Agra Fort.",
"Elizabeth I":"Queen of England (r. 1558–1603), she restored Protestantism, defeated the Spanish Armada and presided over a golden age of literature, including Shakespeare. The East India Company was chartered under her in 1600.",
"Chhatrapati Shivaji":"Founder of the Maratha state (1630–1680), Shivaji built hill forts, a navy and an efficient administration, fought Bijapur and the Mughals and was crowned Chhatrapati in 1674.",
"Peter the Great":"Tsar of Russia (r. 1682–1725) who travelled incognito to western Europe, modernised the army, built a navy and founded St Petersburg.",
"Maharaja Ranjit Singh":"The 'Lion of Punjab' (1780–1839) united Sikh misls and built a strong, secular empire with a modern army.",
"Tipu Sultan":"The 'Tiger of Mysore' (1750–1799) fought the British in four wars, used iron rockets and died defending Srirangapatna.",
"Napoleon Bonaparte":"French general who became emperor (1804), conquered much of Europe and created the Napoleonic Code, before defeat at Waterloo (1815).",
"Queen Victoria":"Queen of the UK (r. 1837–1901) and Empress of India from 1877, whose reign saw industrial and imperial expansion.",
"Rani Lakshmibai":"Queen of Jhansi (c. 1828–1858) who fought the British in the 1857 rebellion after they annexed her kingdom under the Doctrine of Lapse, and died in battle at Gwalior.",
"Mangal Pandey":"A sepoy of the 34th Bengal Native Infantry who attacked his officers at Barrackpore on 29 March 1857; his execution helped spark the rebellion.",
"Mahatma Gandhi":"Mohandas Gandhi (1869–1948) developed satyagraha (non-violent resistance) in South Africa and led India's freedom movement through the Non-Cooperation, Salt March and Quit India campaigns. He was assassinated in 1948.",
"Jawaharlal Nehru":"India's first Prime Minister (1947–1964), he built democratic institutions, planned industry, scientific institutes, and led the Non-Aligned Movement.",
"B. R. Ambedkar":"A Dalit scholar (1891–1956) with doctorates from Columbia and LSE, he chaired the Constituent Assembly's drafting committee and fought caste discrimination.",
"Subhas Chandra Bose":"A Congress president (1938–39) who led the Indian National Army with Japanese support to fight British rule.",
"Bhagat Singh":"A revolutionary (1907–1931) hanged at 23 for shooting a British officer; his writings argued for socialism.",
"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel":"India's first Home Minister, he integrated over 560 princely states into India.",
"Sarojini Naidu":"Poet and freedom fighter, first Indian woman president of Congress (1925).",
"Birsa Munda":"A tribal leader (1875–1900) who led the Ulgulan against British and landlords.",
"Nelson Mandela":"Anti-apartheid leader imprisoned for 27 years who became South Africa's first black president (1994).",
"Martin Luther King Jr.":"Civil rights leader who used non-violent protest inspired by Gandhi.",
"Winston Churchill":"British PM in WWII.",
"Franklin D. Roosevelt":"US president during the Depression and WWII.",
"Adolf Hitler":"Nazi dictator who started WWII and the Holocaust.",
"Joseph Stalin":"Soviet dictator whose purges and famines killed millions.",
"Mao Zedong":"Founder of the People's Republic of China (1949).",
"Confucius":"Kong Fuzi (551–479 BCE) taught ethics, family loyalty, ritual and good government; his sayings were compiled as the Analects and became China's state philosophy under the Han.",
"Laozi":"Traditional author of the Daodejing, founder of Taoism, teaching harmony with the Dao and non-action (wu wei).",
"Gautama Buddha":"Siddhartha Gautama, a Shakya prince, left home to seek an end to suffering, attained enlightenment at Bodh Gaya, and taught the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path for 45 years.",
"Mahavira":"The 24th Jain tirthankara taught non-violence (ahimsa), truthfulness and non-attachment.",
"Socrates":"Athenian philosopher who questioned everything; executed in 399 BCE.",
"Plato":"Student of Socrates who founded the Academy and wrote the Republic.",
"Aristotle":"Plato's student and Alexander's tutor; founded logic and biology.",
"Chanakya":"Kautilya, the Brahmin strategist who helped Chandragupta overthrow the Nandas; traditionally credited with the Arthashastra.",
"Panini":"Grammarian of Gandhara (c. 4th century BCE) whose Ashtadhyayi described Sanskrit in about 4,000 rules, a precursor of formal linguistics and computing.",
"Archimedes":"Greek mathematician of Syracuse who calculated π, discovered buoyancy and designed war machines.",
"Sushruta":"Ancient Indian surgeon whose Sushruta Samhita describes plastic surgery and over 300 procedures.",
"Charaka":"Physician whose Charaka Samhita is a foundation of Ayurveda.",
"Thiruvalluvar":"Tamil poet who wrote the Tirukkural, 1,330 couplets on ethics, wealth and love.",
"Aryabhata":"Mathematician (476–550 CE) who in the Aryabhatiya calculated π to 3.1416, said the Earth rotates, and explained eclipses.",
"Kalidasa":"The greatest classical Sanskrit poet and playwright, author of Abhijnanashakuntalam and Meghaduta.",
"Brahmagupta":"Mathematician (598–668) who gave rules for calculating with zero and negative numbers.",
"Adi Shankaracharya":"Philosopher who taught Advaita Vedanta and founded four mathas.",
"Al-Khwarizmi":"Baghdad scholar whose works gave 'algebra' and 'algorithm' and spread Indian numerals.",
"Ibn Sina (Avicenna)":"Persian physician and philosopher, author of the Canon of Medicine.",
"Al-Biruni":"Scholar who wrote Kitab al-Hind on Indian science and religion.",
"Bhaskara II":"Mathematician whose Lilavati and Siddhanta Shiromani anticipated ideas of calculus.",
"Ibn Battuta":"Moroccan traveller who covered 120,000 km.",
"Kabir":"Weaver-poet who criticised caste and ritual in Hindu and Muslim religion.",
"Guru Nanak":"Founder of Sikhism who taught one God, equality and service.",
"Mirabai":"Rajput princess and Krishna devotee whose songs defied convention.",
"Leonardo da Vinci":"Renaissance painter of the Mona Lisa and inventor.",
"Nicolaus Copernicus":"Proposed the Sun-centred universe.",
"Galileo Galilei":"Used the telescope to support Copernicus; tried by the Inquisition.",
"Isaac Newton":"Formulated the laws of motion and gravity.",
"Charles Darwin":"Proposed evolution by natural selection.",
"Raja Ram Mohan Roy":"Reformer who campaigned against sati and founded the Brahmo Samaj.",
"Savitribai Phule":"India's first woman teacher, who opened girls' schools with Jyotirao Phule.",
"Swami Vivekananda":"Introduced Vedanta to the West at Chicago (1893).",
"Rabindranath Tagore":"First non-European Nobel laureate in literature (1913).",
"Marie Curie":"First person to win two Nobel prizes.",
"Albert Einstein":"Developed relativity.",
"C. V. Raman":"Discovered the Raman effect; Nobel 1930.",
"Srinivasa Ramanujan":"Self-taught mathematical genius.",
"A. P. J. Abdul Kalam":"Missile scientist and President of India.",
}
